ibmveth: fix RX poll hang, reopen hang and netpoll races

Hi,

Four fixes for the ibmveth driver. They apply to net-next as is and
do not depend on the two open() error-path fixes recently applied to
net (af0524bf4ce1, 84bec0bf0352); patch 2 explains how it relates to
them.

  1. Netpoll races with RX replenish: memory corruption and a NULL
     dereference. ndo_poll_controller replenished outside NAPI with
     no lock, so a slot could be posted to PHYP twice, leaking an
     skb and letting PHYP write into an unmapped buffer, and open()
     enabled NAPI before the RX resources existed. The callback is
     removed, as was done for ibmvnic, and NAPI is enabled last.
     Fixes: 6b4223748895, bea3348eef27

  2. Hang after a failed internal reopen. If open() fails during an
     MTU, buffer pool, csum or TSO change, IFF_UP stays set and the
     next close() hangs in napi_disable() with RTNL held; networking
     and shutdown hang and only a reboot recovers.
     Fixes: 860f242eb534

  3. Use-after-free in remove(): a reset queued from NAPI before
     unregister could run on the freed adapter. The reset work is now
     disabled first.
     Fixes: 2c91e2319ed9

  4. RX poll hang on a bad correlator: poll restarted on the same
     slot forever, so the CPU stalled RCU and the reset it queued
     never ran. A correlator naming an inactive buffer pool also
     dereferenced NULL in softirq. Poll now steps past the slot and
     counts the drop in rx_dropped.
     Fixes: 2c91e2319ed9, 860f242eb534

They target net-next rather than net: the triggers are rare (an
open() failure during a direct reopen, PHYP returning a bad
correlator, netconsole during a reconfiguration or under TX
contention, a bad RX slot during remove) and there are no field
reports. All four are suitable for stable once merged; I can repost
them against net if you prefer.

Patches 2 and 4 were requested by the netdev AI review of v7 of the
ibmveth multi-queue RX series [1]; 1 and 3 came out of the follow-up
review of the same code. Taking all four ahead of the multi-queue
series also makes it smaller: the netpoll, reopen and bad-slot
handling become existing behaviour that it extends per queue.

Tested with the ibmveth KUnit suite on qemu pseries (ppc64le); the
new cases in patch 4 fail on the unfixed driver. On a POWER10 LPAR:
netconsole under a printk flood and a ping flood, MTU and buffer
pool changes under traffic, a forced open() failure on an MTU change
followed by down and up, and unbind/bind under traffic. Each commit
message has the details.

[1] https://lore.kernel.org/netdev/cover.1790319558.git.mmc@linux.ibm.com/

